  "name": "Question 19",
  "problem": "Let $B=\\{1,2\\}.$  The relation $R$ on $B$ is given by\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y)\\in {B}^{2}\\,:\\,y-x\\ne 1\\}\n$$\n\n \nExpress the set $R$ as a set of ordered pairs.\n\n$R=\\{(1,1),($\n{{field-1}}$,1),($\n{{field-2}}$,$\n{{field-3}}$)\\}$",
  "worked_solution": "A relation $R$ on a set $A$ is a subset of the Cartesian product $A\\times A={A}^{2}.$\n\n$$\nR⊆A\\times A\n$$\n\nIn this case, $R$ is the set of ordered pairs $(x,y)\\in {B}^{2}$ such that $y-x\\ne 1.$\n\nLet's check each element $x\\in B$ to see which elements $y\\in B$ it could be paired with to satisfy the conditions of our relation.\n\n- If $x=1,$  then the value $y=1$ satisfies $y-x\\ne 1.$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pair $(1,1).$\n\n- If $x=2,$ then the values $y=1$ and $y=2$ satisfy $y-x\\ne 1.$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pairs $(2,1)$ and $(2,2).$\n\nTherefore, we can describe our relation as the following set of ordered pairs:\n\n$$\nR=\\{(1,1),(2,1),(2,2)\\}\n$$",
